WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.899
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:10.160
Let's try to assess the workflow of this piece
of work in the shortest possible form. The

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.389
input is images of cells, and the output of
the algorithm is a decision that tells us

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:18.020
which one of these are cancer cells.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.780
As the pipeline of the entire experiment is
quite elaborate, we'll confine ourselves to

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:26.670
discuss the deep learning-related step at
the very end.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.610
Techniques prior to this one involved adding
chemicals to blood samples.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:34.789
The problem is that these techniques were
not so reliable, and that they also destroyed

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:38.800
the cells, so it was not possible to check
the samples later.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.100
As the title of the paper says, it is a label-free
technique, therefore it can recognize cancer

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.329
cells without any intrusive changes to the
samples. The analysis happens by simply looking

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:50.320
at them.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.769
To even have a chance at saying anything about
these cells, domain experts have designed

00:00:55.769 --> 00:01:00.600
a number of features that help us making an
educated decision. For instance, they like

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.910
to look at refractive indices, that tell us
how much light slows down when passing through

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:12.130
cells. Light absorption and scattering properties
are also recognized by the algorithm. Morphological

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:17.010
features are also quite important as they
describe the shape of the cells and they are

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:20.330
among the most useful features for the detection
procedure.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:26.310
So, the input is an image, then come the high
level features, and the neural networks help

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:31.580
locating the cancer cells by learning the
relation of exactly what values for these

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.880
high-level features lead to cancer cells.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.600
The proposed technique is significantly more
accurate and consistent in the detection than

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.910
previous techniques. It is of utmost importance
that we are able to do something like this

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:50.350
on a mass scale because the probability of
curing cancer depends greatly on which phase

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:56.110
we can identify it. One of the most important
factors is early detection and this is exactly

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:01.570
how deep learning can aid us. To demonstrate
how important early detection is, have a look

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:07.230
at this chart of the ovarian cancer survival
rates as a function of how early the detection

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:13.069
takes place. I think the numbers speak for
themselves, but let's bluntly state the obvious:

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:17.489
it goes from almost surely surviving to almost
surely dying.

00:02:17.769 --> 00:02:22.569
By the way, they were using L2 regularization
to prevent overfitting in the network. We

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:26.980
have talked about what each of these terms
mean in a previous episode, I've put a link

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.280
for that in the description box.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:37.959
95% success rate with the throughput of millions
of cells per second. Wow, bravo. A real, Two

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:41.420
Minute Papers style hat tip to the authors
of the paper.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:47.310
It is really amazing to see different people
from so many areas working together to defeat

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:54.510
this terrible disease. Engineers create instruments
to be able to analyze blood samples, doctors

00:02:54.510 --> 00:03:00.469
choose the most important features, and computer
scientists try to find out the relation between

00:03:00.469 --> 00:03:05.680
the features and illnesses. Great strides
have been made in the last few years, and

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.950
I am super happy to see that even if you're
not a doctor and you haven't studied medicine,

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.629
you can still help in this process. That's
quite amazing.

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:19.739
A big shoutout to Kram who has been watching
Two Minute Papers since the very first episodes

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:24.900
and his presence has always been ample with
insightful comments. Thanks for being around!

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.720
And also, thanks for watching, and for your
generous support, and I'll see you next time!

